<clickhouse>
  <!-- Overrides for the account the image entrypoint creates from CLICKHOUSE_USER,
       merged over users.d/default-user.xml.

       HOW TO APPLY: after ANY change to this file, recreate the ClickHouse
       container with compose, passing its force-recreate flag:

           docker compose up -d clickhouse       (with that flag added)

       Docker binds a single file by inode, and `git pull` replaces it, so the
       running container keeps reading the old one. ClickHouse does reload
       users.d on its own, but only for changes it can see. And a plain `up -d`
       is not enough either: compose sees an unchanged service definition and
       leaves the container alone.

       THE USER NAME IS HARD-CODED AND MUST MATCH CLICKHOUSE_USER. It already was
       before the networks block below. If they diverge, this file configures an
       account that does not exist - silently - and the real admin account keeps
       the entrypoint defaults, which are what this narrows.

       The file name says metabase and has nothing to do with Metabase; renaming
       it means editing the bind mount in docker-compose.yml. -->
  <users>
    <trench_admin>
      <access_management>1</access_management>

      <!-- WHERE THIS ACCOUNT MAY CONNECT FROM.

           The entrypoint writes <ip>::/0</ip>. This account is a superuser with
           grant_option, and port 8123 now has a second door: a systemd socket on
           the host forwards a tailnet address to the container loopback publish
           so an internal mesh can read ClickHouse. Every account authenticates on
           that port, so without this the mesh reached a superuser login prompt.

           replace="replace" IS LOAD-BEARING. ClickHouse merges config trees, so a
           second <networks> block without it APPENDS to ::/0 instead of replacing
           it: the config loads, reads correctly, and restricts nothing.

             host api        the Trench API. A NAME, not an address: container IPs
                             on a bridge network do not survive recreation, and
                             ClickHouse resolves this through Docker DNS.
             127.0.0.1, ::1  `docker exec ... clickhouse-client`, which is how this
                             account is administered.

           Absent on purpose: 172.18.0.1, the bridge gateway, where everything
           arriving through the published port comes from - the mesh proxy, and
           also the read-only `analytics` and `metabase`, which this does not
           touch.

           A REJECTION HERE LOOKS LIKE A WRONG PASSWORD: "password is incorrect,
           or there is no user with such name". Check the restriction before the
           credential:

             SELECT name, host_ip, host_names FROM system.users

           Expected: host_ip=[] and host_names=['localhost','api'] - ClickHouse
           normalises the two loopback literals into the name `localhost`.

           WATCH THE FIRST DEPLOY AFTER THIS LANDS. Deploys recreate `api`, which
           may come up on a different address, and ClickHouse caches DNS - so the
           API can briefly get Authentication failed while the cache is stale.
           Kafka buffers ingest meanwhile. If it recurs, pin the container with
           ipv4_address in docker-compose.yml. -->
      <networks replace="replace">
        <host>api</host>
        <ip>127.0.0.1</ip>
        <ip>::1</ip>
      </networks>
    </trench_admin>
  </users>
</clickhouse>
